GUIDES = {
'how-to-read-option-chain': ('How to read an option chain', 'Understand strikes, expiry, bid/ask spreads, open interest and implied volatility before comparing India or US options.', '''
<p>An option chain groups call and put contracts by strike price and expiration. Start by selecting the correct underlying and expiry. A NIFTY index option and a US ETF option have different contract specifications; a displayed premium alone does not tell you the total cost.</p>
<h2>1. Confirm the contract</h2><p>Check the exchange, underlying, call or put, strike, expiration and contract multiplier. Contract size and settlement rules must come from the exchange or broker. Do not apply a US equity-option multiplier to an Indian index contract.</p>
<h2>2. Read the bid and ask first</h2><p>The bid is the displayed buying interest; the ask is the displayed selling interest. Neither guarantees a fill. The last traded price can be old even when the market has moved. A wide bid/ask spread increases the potential cost of entering and exiting. A missing or crossed quote needs investigation.</p>
<h2>3. Compare activity without guessing direction</h2><p>Volume counts trading activity over the provider's reporting period. Open interest counts outstanding contracts and can update on a different schedule. Every contract has both a buyer and a seller: high call open interest alone does not prove bearish positioning.</p>
<h2>4. Check volatility and time</h2><p>Implied volatility reflects the volatility priced into an option, not a guaranteed forecast. Delta, gamma and theta are model sensitivities that change with price, volatility and time. A correct directional view can still lose money when the premium falls after a volatility event.</p>
<h2>5. Check the source time</h2><p>Celesys separates the time a snapshot was fetched from quote timestamps. When the provider does not supply quote timestamps, freshness remains unverified. Use your broker's current quotes, contract details and event information before making a decision.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.optionseducation.org/optionsoverview/options-basics">Options Industry Council: options basics</a> · <a href="https://www.nseindia.com/option-chain">NSE option chain and explanatory notes</a></p>'''),
'open-interest-and-pcr': ('Open interest and PCR: context, not a prediction', 'Learn what open interest and the put-call ratio can tell you, what they cannot, and why expiry and missing data matter.', '''
<p>Open interest (OI) measures outstanding option contracts. It is different from volume: a contract may trade several times during a session, while open interest depends on whether transactions open or close positions.</p>
<h2>What the put-call ratio measures</h2><p>An OI put-call ratio divides total put open interest by total call open interest for a defined underlying and expiry. A volume put-call ratio uses volume instead. Always label which ratio you mean. If call OI is zero or missing, an OI ratio is undefined.</p>
<h2>A small example</h2><p>For a hypothetical complete expiry with 12,000 put contracts and 10,000 call contracts outstanding, OI PCR is 1.2. This describes the balance of outstanding contracts. It does not mean a 120% chance of a rise, and it does not identify who bought or sold those contracts.</p>
<h2>Why a large OI strike is not a guaranteed wall</h2><p>Positions can be hedges, spreads or directional trades. A large concentration may be useful context, but prices can move through it. Interpreting it requires price behavior, liquidity, changes over time and knowledge of the data source.</p>
<h2>Compare like with like</h2><p>Use the same underlying, expiry, units, strike coverage and update schedule. Comparing a few near-the-money strikes against yesterday's full chain can create a misleading change. Missing OI must remain missing rather than silently becoming zero.</p>
<h2>How Celesys displays it</h2><p>The explorer shows a snapshot OI ratio only when both sides have OI at every returned strike and call OI is positive. Even then, it is calculated from provider-returned contracts, not proof of complete exchange coverage. Celesys does not turn PCR into a standalone buy or sell signal.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.optionseducation.org/optionsoverview/options-basics">Options Industry Council: options basics</a> · <a href="/learn/how-to-read-option-chain">Read the complete option-chain walkthrough</a></p>'''),
'paper-trading-checklist': ('A practical paper-trading checklist', 'Practice a repeatable research and risk-review process, and understand the limits of simulated fills.', '''
<p>Paper trading helps you practice a process without sending real orders. It cannot establish that you will achieve the same fills or results with real capital. Use it to find mistakes in your workflow and to record decisions consistently.</p>
<h2>Before recording an idea</h2><p>Write down the instrument, market, time horizon, evidence timestamp and reason for the idea. Identify what would invalidate the reasoning. Separate observations from assumptions, and explicitly list any missing earnings, economic-event or options data.</p>
<h2>Plan the downside</h2><p>For a stock simulation, compare entry and invalidation levels and calculate the modeled loss per share. A stop is not a guarantee: gaps, liquidity and execution delays can make actual losses larger. Do not transfer underlying-price risk calculations directly to an option premium.</p>
<h2>Record realistic costs</h2><p>Include commissions, spreads and slippage assumptions. Celesys's Trading Desk uses simplified stock/ETF paper fills. Its journal is browser-local, and automatic observations only happen while the desk is running and scanning the relevant symbol. Export the journal if you want to retain it outside that browser.</p>
<h2>Review the process as well as the outcome</h2><p>Ask whether the evidence was available at the decision time, whether you followed your own rules, and whether a result depended on a favorable fill assumption. Track losses and periods without trades alongside winners. A short profitable sample is not a calibrated win probability.</p>
<h2>Know the workspace's limits</h2><p>The desk does not send broker orders. Its stock paper journal does not simulate option fills. Research checks are deterministic rules, and the strategy has no independently audited out-of-sample performance. A WAIT result is a valid outcome when evidence is incomplete.</p>
<p><a href="/trading/">Open the paper-trading desk</a> · <a href="/methodology">Read the data and methodology notes</a></p>''')}
